holds whether or not the code that makes the call looks at it.
}
\details{
A request is checked against its worst case: the most prompt tokens it can
be billed for at the model's input price, plus its output cap as sent (for a
reasoning model the raised cap; see \code{\link[=hr_call]{hr_call()}}) at the output price. The
prompt is counted as the trace counts it, with the JSON schema and extra
body fields the request carries, a few tokens for each message's framing,
and \code{safety_margin} (see \code{\link[=hr_options]{hr_options()}}) on top. One that would take the run
past \code{max_cost_usd} is refused, so a run ends at or under the limit rather
than one request over it. That bound rests on two things the governor
cannot see: that a provider stops a reply at its cap (a
\code{\link[=hr_mock_client]{hr_mock_client()}} or \code{\link[=hr_backend_client]{hr_backend_client()}} handler that returns more is
charged for what it returned), and that the provider counts the prompt
within the margin of the local tokenizer. A provider that reports more
prompt tokens than that, or a tokenizer far from the provider's (the
\code{"heuristic"} one, say), can leave a run a little over the limit. A run
whose requests leave their output cap at the model's limit is stopped while
it still has headroom that a smaller cap would have used.

A refused request is not sent and is not a call: it adds nothing to the
trace's \code{calls}, steps or spend. It comes back from \code{\link[=hr_call]{hr_call()}} as a
failed \link{gr_result} with \code{refused = TRUE} and an error that names the limit,
and a condition of class \code{hr_budget_refused} is signalled, which a caller
can catch with \code{withCallingHandlers()} or \code{tryCatch()} to stop instead. The
trace records the refusal in \code{refusals}, sets \code{budget_stop} and names the
limit in \code{stop_reason}: \code{"calls"}, \code{"cost"}, \code{"tokens"} (the token
ceiling), \code{"output"} (the per-call ceiling), \code{"model"} (not an allowed
model) or \code{"unpriced"}.

A text-generation model with no registered price cannot be held to a
spending limit. harnessr ships no prices, so by default
(\code{allow_unpriced = TRUE}) a request to one is sent while \code{max_cost_usd} is a
finite number, and the trace prices it at \code{NA}, never 0. The first such
request to each model in a session warns, with class \code{hr_unpriced_model},
that the spending limit does not cover the model until you set its prices
with \code{\link[=hr_register_model]{hr_register_model()}} or \code{\link[=hr_load_models]{hr_load_models()}}. \code{max_calls},
\code{max_tokens_total} and the other limits still apply: \code{max_tokens_total} is
the limit that bounds such a run, since the spending limit cannot. With
\code{allow_unpriced = FALSE} the request is refused instead (a condition of
class \code{hr_unpriced_model} as well as \code{hr_budget_refused}), for a run in
which every request must be priced. An embedding model with no price is
neither refused nor warned about here; its requests count against
\code{max_calls} and add nothing to the spend, as \code{\link[=gr_read_many]{gr_read_many()}} warns.
\code{\link[=hr_mock_client]{hr_mock_client()}} makes no paid requests, so it is never refused or warned
about as unpriced. A request an \code{\link[=hr_cache]{hr_cache()}} answers spends nothing
and is checked only against \code{max_calls}, \code{max_output_tokens} and
\code{allowed_models}.

\code{max_tokens_total} holds the tokens of the run: the prompt and reply
tokens of every call the trace records, cache hits included, and the text
sent to be embedded. A request is refused when what the run has used plus
its own worst case (prompt as above, reply at its cap) would pass it.

A governor is attached to a run with \code{hr_trace(governor = )}, and then
governs the requests recorded into that trace. A stage that keeps a trace
of its own and folds it into the one it is given (\code{\link[=gr_read_many]{gr_read_many()}},
\code{\link[=gr_claims]{gr_claims()}}, \code{\link[=gr_outline]{gr_outline()}} and \code{\link[=gr_synthesise]{gr_synthesise()}}) holds its requests to
that trace's governor, less the calls the given trace has already made and
what it has spent and used in tokens, so the governor's limits hold for
the run as a whole. Without a governor, \code{hr_options(max_calls =)} stays a
limit per document for \code{\link[=gr_read_many]{gr_read_many()}} and per stage for the others. A
trace made without one, including those the package makes for itself,
reads the limits from \code{\link[=hr_options]{hr_options()}} each time it checks, so changing an
option affects runs that are already going, as it always has.
}
\section{A reserve for the closing call}{

A reader that works in rounds (the \code{iterative} reader's steps, the
\code{map_reduce} reader's chunks) makes its answer in one last request. When
the rounds use up \code{max_calls} or \code{max_cost_usd}, that request is refused,
and the rounds already paid for end as not found. \code{reserve_calls} and
\code{reserve_usd} hold part of each limit back for such a request. An ordinary
request is refused once it would reach into the reserve: with
\code{max_calls = 10} and \code{reserve_calls = 1}, ordinary requests stop at 9
calls. A request marked as closing may spend the reserve, up to the limit
itself.

A request is marked closing by the code that sends it, with
\code{hr_call(closing = TRUE)}. The readers mark their final request: the
\code{iterative} reader's final answer (\code{iterative.final}), the \code{map_reduce}
reader's last merge (\code{reduce}), the answer request of the
\code{hierarchical}, \code{skim}, \code{preview} and \code{rerank} readers and of the \code{refine}
reader under \code{refine_state = "ledger"}, and the \code{ensemble} reader's
adjudication. The \code{stuff} and \code{retrieve} readers make one model call each,
and the \code{refine} reader's last revision is its answer otherwise; they mark
none. In an ensemble each member's closing
request may spend the reserve too, so hold back one call per member and
one for the adjudication. You set the reserve; nothing a model returns
changes it or marks a request.

Both reserves default to 0, which holds nothing back: requests, refusals
and cache keys are then what they were before the reserve existed. A
reserve larger than its limit is refused with an error of class
\code{hr_bad_reserve}. A reserve with no limit to hold back from (\code{max_calls}
or \code{max_cost_usd} \code{NULL}) has no effect. The requests of a parallel batch
are ordinary: each worker's share is taken from what is left outside the
reserve. Two governors combined (a judge's own and the run's) hold an
ordinary request to the lower of the two limits less their reserves, and a
closing request to the lower of the two limits.
}

\examples{
cl <- hr_mock_client(function(m, p) "ok")
tr <- hr_trace(governor = hr_governor(max_calls = 2))
